  "body": "Three rounds, three executed pairs, all unrunnable; a fourth pair (Triple 8 of 2e242386c0e45309) is queued with no verdict yet. The executed set: Pair 1 (ac1042606b813c15) — descriptive kernel sentence, prose-laden transform, quote-wrapped input; Pair 2 (bc4b975d80aefe10) — bare json-number, append '0', 1e; Pair 3 (e3ad7a1bde02d5d2) — descriptive kernel sentence, bare append 'x', bare hello. The set spans two kernel shapes, two string forms, three inputs; the only common factor is the pipeline. So this proposal adds no third name guess. It pairs with the queued Triple 8 so that the next round, whatever it returns, attributes the fault to a named component.\n\nThe design. Triple 8 runs the bare kernel string equals-'hello' with the parameterized transform append 'x' on hello. This proposal commits the same kernel string, byte-identical, and varies the transform family: a single token, no argument, and an input needing no quotes and no whitespace.\n\nTriple 9. Kernel: equals-'hello' (bare identifier, identical string to Triple 8's). Transform: lowercase. Input: HELLO. Expected motion: reject → accept.\n\nBoundary content, independent of the pipeline question. HELLO differs from the accepted string only in case, and lowercasing is the minimal repair: it touches nothing else. If the pair runs and moves, the kernel is case-sensitive — the first executed fact about any check in this prototype. If it runs and stands still, the kernel case-folds before comparing. That is a reading Triple 8 cannot exclude: a case-folding exact-match still rejects hellox, so Triple 8 moves under both the case-sensitive and the case-folding variant, and only this pair splits them. Run together, the two pairs separate exact-match (both case variants) from starts-with and contains in every combination — with the prepend probe e3ad7a1bde02d5d2 already named for the one surviving ambiguity — or they localize a pipeline fault. No outcome is uninformative. The input is chosen bare and whitespace-free on purpose: a trim probe needs spaces in the input, and Pair 1's quote-wrapped, space-padded input is exactly the kind of string a pipeline can mangle in transit; HELLO cannot be silently altered by whitespace stripping.\n\nThe four outcomes and what each settles.\n- Both run. The bare equals name resolves and both transform forms parse. Pair 3's failure is then pinned on the descriptive kernel string; Pair 1's on its descriptive kernel, prose transform, or quoted input; and Pair 2's on the json-number name itself, since Triple 8 vindicates the append-with-argument form Pair 2 used. The record gains its first executed pairs, and catalogue classification becomes meaningful for the first time. Follow-up: the correction pair (json-number, prepend '-', input '-1', expected accept → reject) — its motion is implementation-independent, and its transform form is by then vindicated.\n- Triple 8 unrunnable, this one runs. The executor is up and the bare equals name is good; the fault is the parameterized transform form, which also explains Pair 2 without implicating any kernel name. Follow-up: json-number with a single-token transform — the only candidate is trim on ' 1 ', whose motion is implementation-dependent (a strict grammar moves, JSON.parse stands) but whose runnability is the signal this stage needs.\n- Triple 8 runs, this one unrunnable. lowercase or HELLO specifically fails to resolve — surprising, and it localizes the fault to the transform registry's accepted names. Follow-up: retry naming (trim, lower) on the same kernel.\n- Both unrunnable. Every guessed form for this kernel has then failed — descriptive in Pairs 1 and 3, bare here — and bare has failed for the other kernel (Pair 2). No further guess from outside can separate executor-down from wrong-names, because the prototype's check list is visible only to the executor. The previous proposal's demand therefore stands, now with three rounds of evidence behind it: an unrunnable verdict must carry